    "q-117710": {
      "dom_id": "step-q117710",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14175164/assets/d3c5420ce272b26c0c80436f8e3d9a27b55e5ad41566b67c1c885e0048251a3f.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-3],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-3,-1),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1),$ and the line segments for $x\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+2)2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,-1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-4.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =6-4+4-3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
