    "q-339677": {
      "dom_id": "step-q339677",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14175164/assets/353412402972b390de950f56b3273d8dbecf60d4d07c7c27e9281c28145a86d2.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The graph consists of $4$ parts: the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [-3,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1],$ the line segment for $x\\in (1,4],$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [4,7].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-1].$ The corresponding region is a combination of a rectangle and a quarter-circle of radius $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)(3)-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =6-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=\\pi -6.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [4,7].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle of radius $3,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =\\pi -6-2+6+\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{13\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi -8}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
